-- !-- Lab Notes -- !--

HYPOTHESIS.  The EGF dictionary of `Applications/CombinatorialSpecies` (sum ↦ sum,
binomial/Day-convolution product ↦ Cauchy product) should *tropicalize*: applying any
additive (Krull) valuation `v : K → WithTop ℤ` coefficient-wise to a counting sequence should
send the species algebra `(+, ⋆)` to the min-plus semiring `(min, +)` of valuation profiles.
We predicted the functor would be only **lax** (≤, not =) because (a) valuations of sums only
see a lower bound (cancellation), and (b) the binomial coefficients `C(n,i)` carry their own
non-negative valuation that lifts the product profile.

EXPERIMENTAL OUTCOME.  Confirmed, fully formally:
  * Sum law `vprofile_add_ge`: `min (vprofile a) (vprofile b) ≤ vprofile (a+b)` — literally the
    coefficient-wise ultrametric inequality `v_add_ge`.
  * Product law `vprofile_binConv_ge`: `tropConv (vprofile a) (vprofile b) ≤ vprofile (binConv a b)`,
    where `tropConv u w n = inf_{i+j=n} (u i + w j)` is the genuine min-plus convolution.
    The proof factors cleanly as: finite ultrametric `v_sum_ge` (valuation of a sum ≥ inf of the
    summands) ∘ pointwise bound `v(C·aᵢ·bⱼ) = v(C)+v(aᵢ)+v(bⱼ) ≥ v(aᵢ)+v(bⱼ)` using
    `v_natCast_nonneg` (every nat coefficient has valuation ≥ 0).
  * The species of sets `E` (all-ones sequence, EGF = exp) has the constant-0 profile, the
    tropical multiplicative unit (`vprofile_const_one`).
  * Concrete model: the p-adic valuation is an `AddVal ℚ` (`padicAddVal`), so every bound is a
    statement about p-adic valuations of binomial convolutions — the tie-in to
    `Computation/PadicValuationDepth`.

INSIGHT.  The right abstraction is the *additive valuation as a lax monoidal functor* from the
GF semiring to the min-plus (tropical) semiring.  `v_natCast_nonneg` is the structural reason
the product law is one-sided: the integer multiplicities of combinatorics live in the
valuation ring `{v ≥ 0}`, so they can only *raise* the Newton-polygon profile.  `tropConv`
is exactly the lower convex hull / Newton-polygon operation in disguise — the bridge says the
Newton polygon of a species product dominates the inf-convolution (Minkowski sum) of the
factors' polygons.

